{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`tests/unit/test_snapshot_submodules_watchdog_c605.py::test_build_with_timed_out_listing_writes_no_sidecar` failed in a local `make verify` on 2026-11-04 (macOS, py3.12, load average about 44) with `SnapshotError: git ls-tree ... timed out after 1s`, raised from the test's SECOND `materialize` call. That call is meant to succeed: the slow `ls-tree` wrapper is already switched off (`slow_ls_tree(False)`), but the `slow_ls_tree` fixture also monkeypatches `git_fetch._GIT_TIMEOUT` to `_BOUND_S` (1 second) for the whole test, so the real `git ls-tree` on the recovery path has a 1-second budget. A loaded host exceeds it. The module passed 3 of 3 standalone runs right after, at the same load, and passed in all 3 CI Python cells for Gerrit 3309 patch set 1.\n\nDiscovered while verifying bald-diagnostic-cricket, whose change (src/rebar/_logging.py) does not touch snapshots; the module passes 3 of 3 with and without that change.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The recovery `materialize` call in the test runs with a git bound that does not depend on host load, and the slow-path assertions still use the 1-second bound.\n- [x] The module passes under synthetic CPU load that previously reproduced the failure."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794491151432203203,"uuid":"bc4d8bc9-a260-4119-a0c9-49c91f2f2cc9"}